    "question": {
      ":question/id": "58ba8c55-b142-52b2-bec2-dcc645819c60",
      ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096700/caller-moderate/generated/0d307bf93f7eb77e91d54f04b99ff9a2edb533dc75f67af1ebaeae5d31cfc286.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $m=-g$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{a}=z$\n\nIII. $g=-\\frac{1}{2}m$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $m$ and $g$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $m$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $g$. So, $m\\ne -g$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $\\mathbf{a}\\ne z$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $g$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $g$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $m$. So, $g=-\\frac{1}{2}m$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b6871fbd-0d3a-5499-8132-53ded7c98e45",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
